  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Wednesday) \nIssued at 1041 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nUnfortunately, the cooler trend through mid-week will be short \nlived, with warmer and drier conditions returning through the \nextended forecast period. The upper level ridge and associated 597 \ndam upper high will expand back eastward through the end of the \nweek, which will spike geopotential heights back above seasonal \nstandards. Thus, resulting in the return of above normal \ntemperatures in the upper 90s to lower triple digits through the \nweekend. Despite the return of unseasonably warm temperatures, highs \nwill remain below extreme heat thresholds and potentially heat \nadvisory criteria as the upper ridge shifts into the Mississippi \nRiver Valley while flattening in response to a deepening shortwave \ntranslating through the Intermountain West region. Nonetheless, it \nwill still be hot each day through the extended and heat safety \nshould still be practiced to avoid heat related illnesses. \n\nAs far as precipitation goes, chances remain slim, with the best \nchance for precipitation being late Tuesday evening as flow aloft \nbecomes southwesterly. Attempting to transport subtropical/monsoonal \nmoisture into the region. However, global NWP guidance keeps the \nflow more southerly if anything, which could limit moisture return \ninto the region. Despite this, ensembles guidance is still hinting \nat at least isolated to scattered activity across the Caprock region \nas disturbances ripple through the main flow and into the area \nTuesday evening. Will opt to maintain NBM mentionable PoPs at this \ntime, although they may need be altered in the coming days as we \nget a better grasp on the set up.",
